# Files that run on Linux keep LF line endings even in a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true. Bash refuses a CRLF script ($'\r': command not found), systemd reads
# the \r into unit values, and a #! line with a trailing \r names an interpreter that does
# not exist. The Linux install is often run straight from a Windows drive, so the working
# copy itself has to be LF, not just the repository.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
linux/** text eol=lf
